text:
`forEach` doesn't wait for promises. It calls your async callback once per file. Each call runs until its first `await`, then returns a promise. `forEach` throws that promise away and moves on to the next file. Once it has started every call, `forEach` returns, and `console.log('done')` runs straight away. The `processFile` calls are still pending at that point, so they finish later.

The `await` inside the callback only pauses that one callback. It doesn't pause `processAll`.

**Fix 1: process the files one at a time, in order**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  for (const f of files) {
    await processFile(f);
  }
  console.log('done');
}
```

**Fix 2: process them all at once and wait for every one**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  await Promise.all(files.map((f) => processFile(f)));
  console.log('done');
}
```

Use the `for...of` loop if the order matters or if `processFile` shouldn't run many times at once, for example when it hits a rate-limited API. Use `Promise.all` if the files are independent and you want the work to finish faster. With `Promise.all`, if any file fails, the `await` rejects as soon as that first failure happens. If you'd rather wait for every file and see each result, use `Promise.allSettled`.
